So, how do you come across? This can sometimes be restricted by persona and perspective, but I like to believe you can make anything work if you try hard enough. Let's start on easy mode...here is a really high level list of tones that I feel truly set the same script, narrative, angle, and even creator/avatar apart. This could make or break performance, depending on whether the tone of your video is communicating the content in a way your audience will be receptive to. 

Tuning in on tones: 
* **Hyped bestie:** this delivery makes the viewer feel like the creator/person doing the voiceover is super excited to be here with them for 60-90s. It tends to be excited, and is best communicated with an on-screen avatar. Body language and facial expressions both matter to effectively deliver this vibe. 
* **Calm educator:** This person is not a know it all. They're probably a nerd, of their chosen kryptonite. Which could be something as fun as reading romance or as practical as purchasing products for a store. The vibe is laid back and informative without lecturing or boring viewers. They should come across as competent and should communicate high interest in the subject without broaching any of the following: over-enthusiastic, raving obsessive, mindless yapper.
* **Casual Hopecore:** So this has a bit less to do with a specific demeanor, and more to do with the emotion they invoke without overly emoting. The topic may be challenging or "negative" but the content should be communicating resolutions, and the overall effect of the primary speaker in the video should be an optimistic realist who is helping viewers figure out their role in bringing about a change. This tone can be especially powerful when you are communicating a potentially controversial topic to a mixed crowd. You won't give skeptics too much to play with by way of emotion, you'll guide already informed viewers towards their next step, and you'll bring in neutral prospects who will most likely read this from an informational lens.
* **Breezy Brand:** So, you want to produce branded content that isn't so...stiff. You want to lean into organic trends witohut losing alignment. What do you do? Keep that slighlty suited up tone that comes across as confident and educational, but play with pacing and language that is less formal. Don't rush, but don't take a breath. This could be delivered by simultaneously showing viewers key aspects of the brand (a sort of listicle, not so clearly spelled) and just adding v.o. for each item/aspect you introduce. String the watcher into the body of the video wiith a longer/shape-shifting list that leads into the heart of the brand storytelling. Here's an example: "If you're interested in {[topic/adjective] or [topic/adjective] or [topic/adjective]} / {[topic/adjective] like this, this, or this} then {you'll love [brand/service]} / [brand/service] [relevance to persona]." Lead from that montage into a script that speaks about the brand. Again, you don't have to follow this listicle hook format, but it's an example to help you see how you can capture attention with a common format while staying true to brand voice. Delivered faster than an austere tone, but slower than the frantic organic trends are commonly delivered.
